    Bug 650623 - Rewrite IMAP tag key generation to produce valid atoms and drop broken MUTF-7. r=BenC


    Historically, custom tag names were converted to IMAP keywords using a Modified UTF-7
    encoder, followed immediately by a lowercase conversion. Because MUTF-7 relies on
    case-sensitive Base64, the forced lowercasing irreversibly corrupted the string for
    any non-ASCII tags (e.g., Cyrillic, Kanji), leading to garbled output on other
    clients (Bug 650623).

    Furthermore, MUTF-7 is strictly defined for folder names, not flags. RFC 3501
    mandates that IMAP flags must be case-insensitive, 7-bit ASCII atoms. The legacy
    MUTF-7 conversion often produced the shift character '&', which is an atom-special
    that crashes strict IMAP parsers (like iCloud) during `-FLAGS` commands.

    This patch completely modernizes Tag Key generation, prioritizing both protocol
    safety and lossless data preservation for international users:
    * Drops the broken MUTF-7 conversion entirely.
    * Introduces a custom "Modified Quoted-Printable" hex encoding (`=XX`) for non-ASCII
      characters, spaces, and IMAP atom-specials. This ensures the resulting key is
      a 100% compliant, server-safe IMAP atom while retaining the exact UTF-8 byte
      sequence for lossless reverse-decoding.
    * Explicitly escapes the `=` character to prevent decoding collisions.
    * Replaces the legacy Netscape-era `A`, `AA`, `AAA` deduplication suffix loop
      with readable integer suffixes (`_1`, `_2`) to gracefully handle naming
      collisions.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D302294

